self_contained_problem:
Setup. Consider an invertible linear system $Ax=b$ with $A\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n}$ a (possibly) nonnormal matrix, i.e., $A^*A\neq AA^*$. Let $S$ denote a relaxation (smoothing) iteration with error-propagation operator $E=I-M^{-1}A$ for some splitting $A=M-N$. An error vector $e\in\mathbb{C}^n$ is called algebraically smooth (with respect to $S$) when relaxation reduces it slowly, i.e., $\|Ee\|\approx\|e\|$. In a Galerkin-type two-grid algebraic multigrid scheme one chooses a restriction operator $R:\mathbb{C}^n\to\mathbb{C}^{n_c}$ and an interpolation operator $P:\mathbb{C}^{n_c}\to\mathbb{C}^n$, and the coarse-grid correction projects errors onto $\mathrm{range}(P)$ in a manner determined by $R$. For the method to converge rapidly, the coarse space $\mathrm{range}(P)$ must accurately approximate the set of algebraically smooth errors, and analogously $\mathrm{range}(R^*)$ must approximate algebraically smooth errors of the adjoint problem $A^*y=c$.

When $A$ is Hermitian (or more generally normal), the algebraically smooth errors are well-characterized as the span of eigenvectors of $A$ associated with small (in modulus) eigenvalues, equivalently the near-kernel of $A$, and this characterization guides the construction of $P$ (and $R$). When $A$ is nonnormal, however, singular vectors of $A$ associated with small singular values do not in general coincide with eigenvectors associated with small-modulus eigenvalues, and neither set need coincide with the algebraically smooth errors of a given relaxation $S$.

Problem. Provide a useful and computationally accessible characterization of the subspace of algebraically smooth errors that the interpolation operator $P$ (respectively the restriction $R$) must approximate on coarse levels of an algebraic multigrid hierarchy when $A$ is a general nonnormal matrix. In particular, identify a subspace $\mathcal{S}(A,S)\subseteq\mathbb{C}^n$ (and a dual subspace $\mathcal{S}^*(A,S)$ for $R$) such that:
(i) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ contains all errors that are slow to be reduced by the smoother $S$ in a meaningful norm;
(ii) approximation of $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ by $\mathrm{range}(P)$ and of $\mathcal{S}^*(A,S)$ by $\mathrm{range}(R^*)$ implies a quantitative two-grid convergence bound bounded away from 1 independently of $n$;
(iii) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ reduces, when $A$ is normal, to the standard near-kernel/small-eigenvalue characterization;
(iv) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ admits practical numerical access (e.g., via test vectors generated by relaxation, suitable Krylov processes, or singular/eigen computations of an associated normal operator).

Let $n\ge 2$ and consider holomorphic self-maps of $\mathbb{C}^n$. A continuous self-map $T$ of a topological space $X$ is called universal (or hypercyclic) if there exists $x\in X$ whose forward orbit $\{T^k(x):k\ge 0\}$ is dense in $X$. One says that $T$ admits a non-trivial invariant Borel probability measure $\mu$ if $\mu(T^{-1}E)=\mu(E)$ for every Borel set $E$ and $\mu$ is not concentrated on a single periodic orbit.

For generic holomorphic self-maps of $\mathbb{C}^n$ (in suitable Baire-category and topological senses), one knows that universality (existence of dense orbits) and the existence of non-trivial invariant Borel probability measures are typical phenomena.

Investigate to what extent the analogous statements hold within the following more restrictive classes of dynamical systems on $\mathbb{C}^n$:

(a) the group $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of biholomorphic automorphisms of $\mathbb{C}^n$;

(b) the subgroup $\mathrm{Aut}_1(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of volume-preserving holomorphic automorphisms of $\mathbb{C}^n$ (those preserving the standard holomorphic volume form $dz_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dz_n$);

(c) for $n$ even, the subgroup $\mathrm{Aut}_\omega(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of holomorphic automorphisms preserving the standard holomorphic symplectic form $\omega=\sum_{j=1}^{n/2} dz_{2j-1}\wedge dz_{2j}$.

Specifically, determine whether a generic (in an appropriate Baire-category sense within each of the above classes equipped with the topology of locally uniform convergence) automorphism $F$ of $\mathbb{C}^n$ in each of these classes:

(i) is universal, i.e. possesses a dense orbit in $\mathbb{C}^n$;

(ii) admits a non-trivial $F$-invariant Borel probability measure on $\mathbb{C}^n$.

Consider a stablecoin system whose value is intended to remain pegged to some target unit of account, and whose backing is provided by a collateral asset under one of the following two regimes:

1. Proxy collateralization: the stablecoin is backed by a separate, on-chain crypto-asset whose market value is exogenous to the stablecoin system.
2. Self collateralization: the stablecoin is backed by an internally issued secondary token of the same system, whose value depends endogenously on the stablecoin's stability and on user expectations.

In both regimes, solvency is enforced by a margin-call mechanism: when the value of posted collateral falls below a contractually specified threshold relative to the outstanding stablecoin liabilities, collateral is liquidated (or additional collateral is required to be posted) so as to restore the required collateralization ratio.

Standard analyses of margin calls assume that the market for the collateral asset is sufficiently deep and liquid that the required quantity of collateral can be sold or acquired in a timely manner without significantly affecting its price. Consider instead settings in which one or more of the following hold:

(a) the collateral market has small total capitalization;
(b) the collateral market is young, with limited trading history and thin order books;
(c) the collateral market is illiquid in the sense that liquidating a non-trivial fraction of outstanding collateral within the time window allowed by the margin-call protocol causes substantial adverse price impact;
(d) market expectations about the future value of the collateral asset deteriorate, so that prospective buyers withdraw and selling pressure increases.

Formulate a mathematical model of the stablecoin, its collateral, the margin-call protocol (including triggering thresholds, liquidation auctions or forced sales, and any feedback between liquidations and collateral price), and the trading environment, that captures conditions (a)–(d). Within such a model, determine whether there exist margin-call mechanisms (parameter choices, auction designs, liquidation schedules, or alternative triggers) such that the stablecoin remains solvent and maintains its peg with high probability under stress, or alternatively prove impossibility results identifying regimes of market size, liquidity, and expectation dynamics in which no margin-call mechanism can guarantee robustness.

A ring $B$ is called semi-graded (SG) if there is a family $\{B_n\}_{n\ge 0}$ of additive subgroups such that $B = \bigoplus_{n\ge 0} B_n$ as abelian groups, $1 \in B_0$, and $B_m B_n \subseteq B_0 \oplus B_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus B_{m+n}$ for all $m,n \ge 0$. Assume that $B$ satisfies the following conditions:
(C1) $B$ is left noetherian;
(C2) $B_0$ is left noetherian;
(C3) each $B_n$ is a finitely generated left $B_0$-module;
(C4) $B_0 \subseteq Z(B)$, the center of $B$.

Let $\mathrm{Gr}\text{-}B$ denote the category of $\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$-filtered (semi-graded) left $B$-modules with the natural notion of morphisms, let $\mathrm{tors}\text{-}B$ be its full subcategory of modules that are direct limits of modules with bounded grading (torsion modules), and let $\mathrm{qgr}\text{-}B$ be the quotient category $\mathrm{Gr}\text{-}B / \mathrm{tors}\text{-}B$, with canonical projection $\pi : \mathrm{Gr}\text{-}B \to \mathrm{qgr}\text{-}B$. For an object $\mathcal{M}$ of $\mathrm{qgr}\text{-}B$, define the section functor $\Gamma(\mathcal{M}) := \bigoplus_{n\ge 0} \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathrm{qgr}\text{-}B}(\pi(B), \mathcal{M}(n))$, where $(n)$ denotes the appropriate shift, and let $\Gamma(\pi(B))_{\ge 0}$ denote the non-negative part of $\Gamma(\pi(B))$.

Suppose further that $B$ satisfies the $\chi_1$ condition: $\mathrm{Ext}^1_B(B/B_{\ge 1}, M)$ is a finitely generated left $B_0$-module for every finitely generated semi-graded left $B$-module $M$.

Question: Does there exist an equivalence of categories
$$\mathrm{qgr}\text{-}B \;\simeq\; \mathrm{qgr}\text{-}\Gamma(\pi(B))_{\ge 0}\,?$$

Consider a living system modeled as a coupled dynamical system whose state variables include (i) a substrate of physical memory structures M (e.g., behavioral engrams encoded in neural connectivity, electrophysiological patterns across cells, or genomic/epigenomic sequences), (ii) a body/architecture B that may undergo radical remodeling (e.g., destruction and rebuilding of neural tissue during metamorphosis, or large-scale tissue regeneration), and (iii) a controller/agent process A that reads from M to produce goal-directed behavior in B over time. An interpretation map I: M × B → Behavior decodes memories into actions in the current bodily context, and the system pursues forward-looking goals G specified by anticipated future states.

Identify a minimal set of dynamical conditions on the triple (M, B, A) and on the interpretation map I that are simultaneously necessary and sufficient for the following property to hold: across episodes of radical structural change of B (with continuity of identity and partial preservation of M), the agent continues to creatively re-interpret M—possibly via novel mappings I' not used before the remodeling—so as to advance forward-looking goals G in the new bodily context. Formally characterize:

(a) the information-theoretic and dynamical invariants of M that must be preserved under remodeling of B;
(b) the closure or self-referential properties of A required for it to generate new interpretation maps I' rather than only fixed decodings;
(c) the coupling (in particular, the role of bioelectric / electrophysiological dynamics across all cells, not only excitable nerve and muscle cells) between M, B, and A that is necessary and sufficient for goal-directed creative reinterpretation across major morphological transitions.

Provide a proof of necessity and sufficiency, or rigorous counterexamples delimiting which conditions can be weakened.

Develop a rigorous computational framework for simulating self-modifying open systems, i.e., dynamical computational systems in which not only the state but also the defining program, operators, structural relations, transition rules, and even the representational categories (the types, state spaces, or ontologies in which the system is described) are themselves time-varying objects subject to update by the system's own dynamics and by interaction with an environment.

Concretely, identify and characterize modeling paradigms and formal tools (for example: reflective and metacircular interpreters, higher-order rewriting systems, graph and hypergraph rewriting, membrane/P-systems, category-theoretic models such as functorial semantics with evolving signatures, open game/lens-based compositional semantics, agent-based and multi-level simulation frameworks, type systems supporting dynamic extension, or coalgebraic models of open systems) that can:

(i) represent a system S whose description ⟨P_t, Σ_t, R_t, C_t⟩ — program P_t, signature/operator set Σ_t, rule set R_t, and representational category C_t — is itself a function of time t;
(ii) admit a well-defined operational semantics in which transitions may rewrite components of ⟨P_t, Σ_t, R_t, C_t⟩, including changes of the ambient category C_t, while preserving a coherent notion of identity and history of S;
(iii) support openness, meaning interaction with an environment whose interface may also be renegotiated at runtime;
(iv) permit effective implementation, i.e., be realizable as an executable simulator on a standard computational substrate.

Provide such a paradigm together with theorems establishing its expressiveness, compositionality, and adequacy for modeling self-modifying open systems, or prove obstructions showing that no paradigm in a specified class can satisfy (i)–(iv) simultaneously.

Consider a dynamical system whose state at time t is described not only by a configuration x_t in some state space X, but also by a structure S_t belonging to a space S of admissible structures (e.g., interaction networks, sets of components, or rule schemata), and by a rule r_t ∈ R that governs how S_t evolves, i.e., S_{t+1} = r_t(S_t, x_t). Suppose further that r_t itself evolves according to a meta-rule m_t ∈ M, with r_{t+1} = m_t(r_t, S_t, x_t), and that m_t may in turn evolve according to higher-order rules, yielding a hierarchy (x_t, S_t, r_t, m_t, m^{(2)}_t, …) of states, structures, and rules at successive levels.

Develop a practical mathematical and computational framework for modeling such hierarchies of evolutionary change, in which:
(a) changes to the rules generating structural change in the system can be represented and simulated;
(b) the framework supports an arbitrary (or at least extendable) number of meta-levels;
(c) it admits inference, prediction, or qualitative analysis of trajectories from observed data of biological or social systems undergoing transformation;
(d) it reduces, at the lowest level, to standard models of state evolution on a fixed structure with fixed rules.

Specifically, propose explicit constructions (for example based on category-theoretic, type-theoretic, stochastic-process, or open-ended evolutionary computation formalisms) and demonstrate that the resulting models can capture transformations that are not expressible as evolution under a fixed rule set on a fixed structure space.

Let $V=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\infty} V_i$ be a graded vector space over a field $k$, and let $D\colon V\to V$ be a graded linear operator of degree $-1$, that is, $D(V_0)=\{0\}$ and $D(V_{i+1})\subseteq V_i$ for all $i\geq 0$. 

Determine whether it is always possible to equip $V$ with a bilinear product $V\times V\to V$ such that:
(i) the product makes $V$ into a graded $k$-algebra (i.e. $V_i\cdot V_j\subseteq V_{i+j}$);
(ii) the product is associative;
(iii) the algebra has a multiplicative identity $1\in V_0$;
(iv) $D$ is a derivation for this product, that is, $D(ab)=D(a)\,b+a\,D(b)$ for all $a,b\in V$.

Equivalently, characterize those pairs $(V,D)$ as above for which such an associative graded unital algebra structure on $V$ making $D$ a derivation exists.

Let (K, v) be a valued field, with value group vK, residue field Kv, and let K^{ac} denote an algebraic closure of K equipped with the unique extension of v. An algebraic extension (L|K, v) of a henselian valued field (K, v) is called tame if for every finite subextension K'|K of L|K the following three conditions hold:

(T1) the ramification index (vK' : vK) is not divisible by the characteristic of the residue field Kv;
(T2) the residue field extension K'v | Kv is separable;
(T3) the extension (K'|K, v) is defectless, i.e., [K' : K] = (vK' : vK) · [K'v : Kv].

The valued field (K, v) is called a tame field if (K^{ac} | K, v) is a tame extension. It is known that the theory of tame fields is model complete in the language L_val of valued rings, relatively to the theories of the value group vK in the language of ordered abelian groups and of the residue field Kv in the language of rings.

Problem: Determine whether every tame field admits quantifier elimination in some suitable (possibly enriched) language extending L_val together with the natural languages on the value group and residue field sorts. Equivalently, find such a language and prove quantifier elimination, or show that no such enrichment can yield quantifier elimination for the class of tame fields.

Let p be a prime number. Equip each of the following fields with its canonical valuation (the unique extension of the p-adic valuation on Q_p in cases (a)–(c), and the t-adic valuation in cases (d)–(e)):

(a) Q_p(ζ_{p^∞}) := ⋃_{n≥1} Q_p(ζ_{p^n}), where ζ_{p^n} is a primitive p^n-th root of unity;
(b) Q_p(p^{1/p^∞}) := ⋃_{n≥1} Q_p(p^{1/p^n}), where the p^{1/p^n} form a compatible system of p^n-th roots of p;
(c) Q_p^{ab}, the maximal abelian extension of Q_p inside a fixed algebraic closure;
(d) F_p((t))^{1/p^∞} := ⋃_{n≥0} F_p((t))^{1/p^n}, the perfect hull of F_p((t));
(e) F_p^{ac}((t))^{1/p^∞}, the perfect hull of F_p^{ac}((t)), where F_p^{ac} denotes an algebraic closure of F_p.

Let L_val denote the first-order language of valued rings (the language of rings together with a binary predicate for the valuation divisibility relation), and let L_val(t) denote L_val enlarged by a constant symbol naming t.

Determine whether the following theories are decidable:
(1) the L_val-theory of Q_p(ζ_{p^∞});
(2) the L_val-theory of Q_p(p^{1/p^∞});
(3) the L_val-theory of Q_p^{ab};
(4) the L_val-theory and the L_val(t)-theory of F_p((t))^{1/p^∞};
(5) the L_val-theory and the L_val(t)-theory of F_p^{ac}((t))^{1/p^∞}.

Let (K, v) be a valued field with valuation ring O_K, value group vK, and residue field Kv. Call (K, v) extremal if for every n ≥ 1 and every polynomial f(X_1, …, X_n) ∈ K[X_1, …, X_n], the set
  { v(f(a_1, …, a_n)) : a_1, …, a_n ∈ O_K } ⊆ vK ∪ {∞}
attains a maximum.

A valuation w on K is called a coarsening of v if the valuation ring of w contains the valuation ring of v (equivalently, every v-open set is w-open). Given such a coarsening w with residue field Kw, the valuation v induces a valuation w̄ on Kw whose valuation ring is O_K / m_w (where m_w is the maximal ideal of the valuation ring of w). One writes v = w ∘ w̄ and calls v the composition of w and w̄; the residue field of (Kw, w̄) is canonically identified with Kv.

Question: Suppose that (K, v) admits a decomposition v = w ∘ w̄ such that:
 (i) the value group wK is divisible;
 (ii) the valued field (K, w) is extremal;
 (iii) the valued field (Kw, w̄) is extremal.

Does it follow that (K, v) is extremal?

For an absolutely convergent series of real numbers $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n$, define its achievement set (set of subsums) by
$$E(x_n) := \Big\{\, y \in \mathbb{R} : \exists\, A \subseteq \mathbb{N} \text{ such that } y = \sum_{n \in A} x_n \,\Big\}.$$
It is a classical fact that, when $(x_n)$ is a sequence of nonzero real numbers tending to $0$ such that the series converges absolutely, $E(x_n)$ is a non-empty compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$ that must be exactly one of the following four topological types:
(i) a finite set;
(ii) a finite union of nondegenerate closed intervals;
(iii) homeomorphic to the Cantor ternary set (a "Cantor set");
(iv) a Cantorval, i.e., a non-empty compact perfect set $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ such that both endpoints of every connected component of $C$ are accumulation points of one-point components of $C$, and such that $C$ has nontrivial interior but is not a finite union of intervals.

Problem. Provide a complete characterization, expressed directly in terms of the generating sequence $(x_n)$, that determines which of the four topological types (i)–(iv) the achievement set $E(x_n)$ belongs to. In particular, give necessary and sufficient conditions on $(x_n)$ for $E(x_n)$ to be (a) a finite set, (b) a finite union of closed intervals, (c) a Cantor set, and (d) a Cantorval.

Let $f:\widehat{\mathbb{C}}\to\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ be a rational map of degree at least $2$ acting on the Riemann sphere. The Fatou set $\mathcal{F}(f)$ is the largest open set on which the iterates $\{f^{\circ n}\}_{n\ge 1}$ form a normal family, and the Julia set is its complement. A Fatou component $U$ (a connected component of $\mathcal{F}(f)$) is called a Herman ring if it is conformally equivalent to a round annulus $A_r=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:1<|z|<r\}$ for some $r>1$ and the first-return map of $f$ on $U$ is conformally conjugate to an irrational rotation $z\mapsto e^{2\pi i\theta}z$ of $A_r$.

A root-finding method associated to a polynomial (or entire function) $p$ is a rational (resp. meromorphic) map $T_p$ designed so that the roots of $p$ are attracting fixed points of $T_p$. Standard examples include:
- Newton's method: $N_p(z)=z-\frac{p(z)}{p'(z)}$;
- König's method of order $k\ge 2$: $K_{p,k}(z)=z+(k-1)\frac{(1/p)^{(k-2)}(z)}{(1/p)^{(k-1)}(z)}$;
- Chebyshev's method: $C_p(z)=z-\left(1+\frac{1}{2}\frac{p(z)p''(z)}{p'(z)^2}\right)\frac{p(z)}{p'(z)}$.

Question: Does there exist a polynomial (or entire function) $p$ and a root-finding method $T_p$ — for instance one of Newton, König, or Chebyshev type, or a generalization thereof — such that the Fatou set $\mathcal{F}(T_p)$ contains a Herman ring?

Consider a planet of mass $M_p$, radius $R_p$, and orbital semi-major axis $a$ around a host star of mass $M_\star$ that produces stellar flares. A flare event is characterized by a bolometric energy $E_f$, a time-resolved spectral energy distribution $F_\lambda(t)$ spanning X-ray, EUV, UV, and optical wavelengths, and an associated solar/stellar energetic particle (SEP) flux $\Phi_p(E,t)$ with energy spectrum in $E$ (protons and heavier ions). Assume the planet possesses an atmosphere with surface pressure $P_0$, composition vector $\{X_i\}$ (mole fractions of species $i$), and (optionally) a global magnetic dipole moment $\mathcal{M}$.

Develop a quantitative theoretical and computational framework that, given the flare statistics (occurrence frequency-energy distribution $dN/dE_f \propto E_f^{-\alpha}$ over the stellar lifetime), the spectral and particle properties of individual flare events, and the planetary parameters above, determines:

(a) the rate of atmospheric mass loss due to thermal (XUV-driven hydrodynamic) and non-thermal (ion pickup, sputtering, photochemical) escape processes induced by repeated flaring, and the conditions under which the atmosphere is retained over geological timescales;

(b) the steady-state and transient atmospheric chemical composition, including depletion of ozone or analogous UV-shielding species, production of $\mathrm{NO}_x$, $\mathrm{HO}_x$, and other photochemical and SEP-induced byproducts, as a function of the flare energy distribution and SED hardness;

(c) the surface ultraviolet and ionizing radiation dose received by the planet during and between flares, accounting for atmospheric attenuation and (where relevant) magnetospheric shielding from SEPs, and the consequent implications for surface conditions and biosignature stability.

Characterize the parameter regimes (in $E_f$, $\alpha$, $a$, $P_0$, $\{X_i\}$, $\mathcal{M}$) that delineate habitable from non-habitable outcomes under sustained flare forcing.

Consider the following observational situation in extragalactic astrophysics. A growing sample of star-forming galaxies at high redshift (z ≳ 6, in the reionization era) shows nebular emission-line spectra implying nitrogen-to-oxygen abundance ratios log(N/O) that are markedly elevated compared to the bulk of local star-forming galaxies and H II regions at comparable metallicity. In the (log(N/O), log(O/H)) plane, these galaxies cluster between the equilibrium abundance pattern of the CN cycle and that of the full CNO cycle of hydrogen burning, a locus that can in principle be reached at normal C/O if material processed through partial CNO burning is selectively expelled.

Identify and discriminate among the following candidate physical mechanisms that could account for this strong N/O enhancement:

(a) unusually efficient and bursty star formation histories, which can transiently raise N/O via differential timescales of N and O production;
(b) the galaxies being observed progenitors of present-day globular clusters, in which the conditions for the multiple-population N enhancement seen in old globular clusters are operating;
(c) enrichment driven by extremely massive stars (M ≳ a few hundred M_⊙) or supermassive stars (M ≳ 10^3–10^5 M_⊙) whose hot-bottom or interior CNO-processed material is shed via winds;
(d) enrichment by ordinary massive stars (≈ 8–100 M_⊙) and very massive stars (≈ 100–300 M_⊙) through stellar winds and binary mass transfer, with no need for an exotic population;
(e) systematic biases in the nebular abundance diagnostics, stellar-evolution tracks, or stellar-feedback prescriptions used to interpret the spectra.

Taking into account that high-redshift gas-phase metallicities are determined almost exclusively from oxygen lines, while stellar population-synthesis models adopted to interpret the ionizing continuum depend sensitively on the iron abundance (which is decoupled from oxygen by the differing nucleosynthetic timescales of core-collapse and Type Ia supernovae), determine which of (a)–(e), or which combination thereof, is required to consistently reproduce the observed N/O versus O/H locus and the associated UV/optical line ratios of the high-redshift N-rich galaxies.

Design a methodological framework that robustly connects integrated observations of massive stars in high-redshift galaxies—where spatial resolution is limited and only restricted wavelength regimes are accessible—to the underlying stellar-physical processes (e.g. mass loss, rotation, binarity, ionizing-photon production, chemical yields) by means of a hierarchical "ladder" of local analogs and testbed environments. Concretely, specify how to combine and cross-calibrate the following rungs so that detailed stellar physics and population-synthesis constraints derived locally can be transferred to integrated high-redshift measurements:

(i) nearby low-metallicity dwarf galaxies treated as integrated-light analogs of high-redshift systems;
(ii) unresolved young massive clusters in the Local Volume, used to constrain population-synthesis models at the cluster scale;
(iii) resolved young massive clusters in which individual stars can be characterized spectroscopically while the cluster environment supplies additional empirical constraints (age, mass function, multiplicity statistics) that anchor population-synthesis testbeds;
(iv) individual stellar systems at very low metallicity, such as iron-poor O-type stars in the Magellanic Bridge, providing direct stellar-physics calibrators.

The problem is to define quantitative procedures—sample selection criteria, observable diagnostics, model ingredients, and statistical inference techniques—that propagate physical constraints from rungs (iv) → (iii) → (ii) → (i) → high-redshift integrated spectra in a controlled and falsifiable way, and to identify which observables and population-synthesis assumptions dominate the systematic uncertainty in this propagation.

Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ be symmetric and let $X_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times b}$ be a starting block with orthonormal columns. The block Lanczos algorithm generates, for $k=1,2,\ldots$, blocks $X_k \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times b}$ and $b\times b$ matrices $M_k$ (symmetric) and $B_k$ (upper triangular) via the three-term recurrence
$$ A X_k = X_{k-1} B_{k-1}^T + X_k M_k + X_{k+1} B_k, $$
where $M_k = X_k^T A X_k$ and $X_{k+1} B_k$ is obtained by orthogonalizing $A X_k - X_{k-1} B_{k-1}^T - X_k M_k$ against $X_k$ (with $X_0 := 0$, $B_0 := 0$) and then performing an intra-block QR-type orthogonalization to produce $X_{k+1}$ with orthonormal columns and the upper-triangular factor $B_k$. After $k$ steps, the computed block tridiagonal matrix
$$ T_k = \begin{pmatrix} M_1 & B_1^T & & \\ B_1 & M_2 & B_2^T & \\ & B_2 & \ddots & \ddots \\ & & \ddots & M_k \end{pmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{kb \times kb} $$
yields approximate eigenvalues (Ritz values) of $A$.

In the single-vector case $b=1$, Paige's classical finite-precision analysis establishes rigorous componentwise bounds on the loss of local orthogonality, the size of the residual $A\hat{X}_k - \hat{X}_{k-1}\hat{B}_{k-1}^T - \hat{X}_k \hat{M}_k - \hat{X}_{k+1}\hat{B}_k$, the deviation $\hat{X}_k^T \hat{X}_k - I$, and the consequences for Ritz value behavior, all in terms of the unit roundoff $\varepsilon$, the dimensions, and $\|A\|$.

Problem. Develop an analogue of Paige's finite-precision analysis for the block Lanczos algorithm. Specifically, under either (a) explicit, quantitative assumptions on the accuracy of the intra-block orthogonalization scheme used to produce each $\hat{X}_{k+1}$ and $\hat{B}_k$ (e.g.\ bounds of the form $\|\hat{X}_{k+1}^T \hat{X}_{k+1} - I\| \le c\,\varepsilon$ and $\|\hat{X}_{k+1}\hat{B}_k - W_k\| \le c\,\varepsilon\|W_k\|$ for the input $W_k$), or (b) the choice of a specific standard orthogonalization scheme (such as Householder QR or twice-modified Gram–Schmidt), prove rigorous bounds in terms of $\varepsilon$, $b$, $k$, $n$, and $\|A\|$ on:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the residual of the block three-term recurrence;
\item the loss of orthogonality $\|\hat{X}_j^T \hat{X}_k\|$ both within blocks and between blocks;
\item the size and structure of the perturbations needed so that the computed $T_k$ may be interpreted as arising from an exact block Lanczos process applied to a nearby larger symmetric matrix; and
\item the implications for the accuracy of the computed Ritz values as approximations to eigenvalues of $A$.
\end{enumerate}

Consider the following two typed calculi, each equipped with a semantic notion of subtyping (i.e., subtyping defined as set-theoretic containment of the denotations of types, where types are interpreted as sets of values).

(1) Duce is a call-by-value λ-calculus extended with overloaded (intersection-typed) functions and recursive types. Its types are built from basic types, products, and arrow types using the boolean connectives ∨, ∧, ¬, and a recursion operator μ. Subtyping t ≤ s is defined semantically: t ≤ s iff every value belonging to t belongs to s. In particular, an overloaded function with type ⋀_{i∈I}(t_i → s_i) accepts arguments in ⋁_i t_i and dispatches according to the most specific arrow.

(2) The local π-calculus π is the asynchronous π-calculus with a type system featuring input/output channel-capability types ch^+(t) (output-only), ch^−(t) (input-only), and ch(t) (both), together with the same boolean and recursive type constructors as Duce, and semantic subtyping on these types. In the local discipline, only the output capability of a channel can be transmitted, so received channels carry only ch^+(t) types.

Problem. Define a translation ⟦·⟧ from Duce terms to π processes and a translation |·| from Duce types to π types that is:
 (a) type-preserving: if Γ ⊢ M : t in Duce, then ⟦Γ⟧ ⊢ ⟦M⟧ : |t| in π (under a suitable continuation-passing convention);
 (b) subtyping-preserving: t ≤_Duce s implies |t| ≤_π |s|;
 (c) sound for overloaded functions: the dispatch semantics of intersection-typed Duce functions is faithfully simulated by the π processes;
 (d) compatible with recursive types under the translation; and
 (e) reconciles the "channel-type gap" between the natural Duce arrow translation, which would require a fully bidirectional channel type ch^λ(t) for the function-argument channel, and the local-π discipline, which only provides ch^−(t) on the receiving side.

Either exhibit such an encoding or prove that no encoding satisfying (a)–(e) exists, in which case formulate and develop instead an integrated concurrent extension of Duce (a single calculus combining higher-order functional computation with overloading and recursive types together with π-style concurrency) in which the desired typing and subtyping properties hold natively.

For each of the following four three-dimensional autonomous quadratic ODE systems on $\mathbb{R}^3$, where $A\in\mathbb{R}$ is a real parameter, give a rigorous proof of the existence of a chaotic solution (e.g., the existence of an invariant set on which the flow is topologically conjugate, or semi-conjugate, to a shift on a finite alphabet, exhibiting positive topological entropy of Rössler-attractor type):

(i) $x' = y^2 - x + Az,\quad y' = x,\quad z' = y$;

(ii) $x' = yz - x + Ay,\quad y' = z,\quad z' = x$;

(iii) $x' = yz + Az,\quad y' = x - y,\quad z' = y$;

(iv) $x' = y^2 + Az,\quad y' = x - y,\quad z' = y$.

Numerical evidence indicates that, for suitable values of $A$, each system possesses a Rössler-type strange attractor. The task is to produce a rigorous (e.g., computer-assisted topological or analytic) proof of chaos for each of the four systems.

Let $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_n$ be a finite word of length $n$ over the binary alphabet $\{0,1\}$. A run (also called a maximal repetition) in $w$ is a triple $(i, j, p)$ with $1 \le i \le j \le n$ and a positive integer $p$ such that:
(i) the factor $w_i w_{i+1} \cdots w_j$ has period $p$ (meaning $w_k = w_{k+p}$ for all $i \le k \le j-p$);
(ii) $2p \le j - i + 1$ (the factor has exponent at least $2$);
(iii) the period and the occurrence are maximal, i.e. neither $w_{i-1} w_i \cdots w_j$ (if $i > 1$) nor $w_i \cdots w_{j+1}$ (if $j < n$) has period $p$.

Let $\rho(w)$ denote the number of runs in $w$, and let $\rho(n) = \max\{\rho(w) : w \in \{0,1\}^n\}$.

Prove or disprove: $\rho(n) < n$ for every positive integer $n$.

A trisection of a closed connected oriented smooth 4-manifold $M$ is a decomposition $M = X_1 \cup X_2 \cup X_3$ such that:
(i) each $X_i$ is a 4-dimensional handlebody, i.e. diffeomorphic to a boundary connected sum $\natural_{k_i}(S^1 \times D^3)$ for some integer $k_i \ge 0$ (the case $k_i = 0$ being the 4-disk $D^4$);
(ii) the pairwise intersections $X_i \cap X_j$ ($i \ne j$) are 3-dimensional handlebodies;
(iii) the triple intersection $\Sigma = X_1 \cap X_2 \cap X_3$ is a closed orientable surface, called the central surface of the trisection.
The genus of the piece $X_i$ is the integer $k_i$ above.

Question: Does every closed simply-connected smooth 4-manifold admit a trisection in which at least one of the three 4-dimensional pieces $X_i$ has genus $0$ (equivalently, is diffeomorphic to the 4-disk $D^4$)?

Let $\mathbb{T}^1 = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ and consider the closed annulus $A = \mathbb{T}^1 \times [-1,1]$ equipped with Lebesgue measure. Let $f \colon A \to A$ be a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism such that:

(i) $f$ preserves Lebesgue measure;
(ii) $f$ is homotopic to the identity;
(iii) $f$ has only finitely many periodic points;
(iv) the restriction of $f$ to each boundary circle $\mathbb{T}^1 \times \{\pm 1\}$ is a circle diffeomorphism with the same rotation number $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$, and $\alpha$ is Diophantine (i.e., there exist constants $C>0$ and $\tau \geq 0$ such that $|\alpha - p/q| \geq C/q^{2+\tau}$ for every $p/q \in \mathbb{Q}$).

Must $f$ be $C^\infty$-conjugate to the rigid annulus translation
$$R_\alpha(\theta, r) = (\theta + \alpha, r) ?$$

Let $D^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ denote the closed two-dimensional disk, equipped with a smooth area form whose induced probability measure is $\mu$. Let $\mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$ be the space of $C^1$ diffeomorphisms of $D^2$ that preserve $\mu$, endowed with the $C^1$ topology. For $f \in \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$, define the maximal integrated Lyapunov exponent by
$$ \lambda^+(f,\mu) \;=\; \int_{D^2} \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{n} \log \| Df^n_x \| \, d\mu(x), $$
and let $h_\mu(f)$ denote the metric (Kolmogorov–Sinai) entropy of $f$ with respect to $\mu$. Set
$$ \mathcal{G} \;=\; \{\, f \in \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2) : \lambda^+(f,\mu) = 0 \,\}. $$
Decide which of the following two mutually exclusive alternatives holds:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $\mathcal{G}$ is a dense $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$;
\item[(b)] there exist a nonempty $C^1$-open set $\mathcal{U} \subset \mathrm{Diff}^1_\mu(D^2)$ and a constant $\delta > 0$ such that $h_\mu(g) \geq \delta$ for every $g \in \mathcal{U}$.
\end{itemize}

Let $M$ be a compact connected smooth manifold (without boundary), and let $f : M \to M$ be a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. Recall that:
- $f$ is called minimal if every orbit $\{f^n(x) : n \in \mathbb{Z}\}$ is dense in $M$;
- $f$ is called uniquely ergodic if there exists exactly one $f$-invariant Borel probability measure on $M$;
- $f$ is called strictly ergodic if it is both minimal and uniquely ergodic;
- the topological entropy $h_{\mathrm{top}}(f)$ is defined, for any compatible metric $d$ on $M$, as
$$h_{\mathrm{top}}(f) = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0}\, \limsup_{n\to\infty}\, \frac{1}{n} \log s(n,\varepsilon),$$
where $s(n,\varepsilon)$ is the maximal cardinality of an $(n,\varepsilon)$-separated subset of $M$ with respect to the Bowen metric $d_n(x,y)=\max_{0\le k<n} d(f^k x, f^k y)$.

Question. If $f$ is a strictly ergodic $C^1$ diffeomorphism of a compact connected manifold $M$, must one have $h_{\mathrm{top}}(f) = 0$?

A Hardy field is a closed-under-differentiation, field of germs at $+\infty$ of real-valued functions defined on some half-line $[t_0,\infty)$. Fix a Hardy field $\mathcal H$ and let $F=\{a_1,\dots,a_\ell\}\subset\mathcal H$ consist of functions of polynomial growth (i.e., for each $i$ there exists $k\in\mathbb N$ with $a_i(t)/t^k\to 0$). Denote by $\mathrm{span}^*(F)$ the set of all nontrivial real linear combinations $a=c_1a_1+\cdots+c_\ell a_\ell$ with $(c_1,\dots,c_\ell)\in\mathbb R^\ell\setminus\{0\}$.

Say that the family of integer-part sequences $\{([a_1(n)]),\dots,([a_\ell(n)])\}$ is *good for $\ell$-fold mean convergence (for a single transformation)* if for every invertible measure-preserving system $(X,\mathcal B,\mu,T)$ and every $f_1,\dots,f_\ell\in L^\infty(\mu)$, the averages
$$\frac1N\sum_{n=1}^{N} T^{[a_1(n)]}f_1\cdot T^{[a_2(n)]}f_2\cdots T^{[a_\ell(n)]}f_\ell$$
converge in $L^2(\mu)$ as $N\to\infty$.

Prove or disprove: the family $\{([a_1(n)]),\dots,([a_\ell(n)])\}$ is good for $\ell$-fold mean convergence if and only if every function $a\in\mathrm{span}^*(F)$ satisfies at least one of the following three asymptotic alternatives:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $|a(t)-c\,p(t)|/\log t\to\infty$ as $t\to\infty$ for every $c\in\mathbb R$ and every $p\in\mathbb Z[t]$;
\item[(ii)] $a(t)-c\,p(t)\to d$ as $t\to\infty$ for some $c,d\in\mathbb R$ and some $p\in\mathbb Z[t]$;
\item[(iii)] $|a(t)-t/m|\le C\log t$ for all sufficiently large $t$, for some nonzero $m\in\mathbb Z$ and some constant $C>0$.
\end{itemize}

Consider a nonlinear Schrödinger equation (NLS) of the form
   i u_t + Δu + f(|u|^2) u = 0,
posed on a compact spatial domain (for instance the d-dimensional torus T^d, or a bounded domain with appropriate boundary conditions), where f is a smooth nonlinearity for which the Cauchy problem is globally well-posed in the Sobolev space H^m for some integer m ≥ 1.

For an initial datum u_0 ∈ H^m, let u(t,·) denote the corresponding global solution, with u(0,x) = u_0(x). For 0 ≤ s < m denote by ‖·‖_m the H^m-norm and let H^s denote the lower-regularity Sobolev space.

Determine which of the following long-time behaviors hold for solutions arising from a "typical" initial condition u_0 ∈ H^m (in the sense of being generic, e.g. on a residual set, or with full measure with respect to a natural reference measure on H^m):

 (a)  lim_{t→∞} ‖u(t)‖_m = ∞;
 (a') limsup_{t→∞} ‖u(t)‖_m = ∞;
 (b)  u(t) ⇀ 0 weakly in H^s as t → ∞;
 (b') there exists a sequence t_n → ∞ such that u(t_n) ⇀ 0 weakly in H^s.

The problem asks to identify which (if any) of the properties (a), (a'), (b), (b') are satisfied by typical solutions of the NLS.

Setup. Let $\mathbb{N}_0=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ and let $\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ denote the space of bounded scalar sequences $x=(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)$. A linear subspace $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)\subseteq\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ equipped with a quasi-norm $\|\cdot\|_{\mathfrak{z}}$ is called a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal if:
  (i) $(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0),\|\cdot\|_{\mathfrak{z}})$ is complete;
  (ii) it contains the canonical unit sequences;
  (iii) (monotonicity) whenever $x\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ and $y\in\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ satisfy $|\eta_k|\le|\xi_k|$ for all $k$, then $y\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ with $\|y\|_{\mathfrak{z}}\le\|x\|_{\mathfrak{z}}$;
  (iv) (shift-invariance/monotonicity under shifts) the backward shift $S_-:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)\mapsto(\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)$ and the forward shift $S_+:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)\mapsto(0,\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)$ both map $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ into itself continuously.

Consider the bounded linear operator
$$T:=I-2S_-:\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)\longrightarrow\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0),\qquad T(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)=(\xi_0-2\xi_1,\;\xi_1-2\xi_2,\;\xi_2-2\xi_3,\dots),$$
and let $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z}):=T(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0))$ denote its range. The following mutually exclusive cases for this range are a priori possible:
  Case 1. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed and equal to $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ (codimension $0$);
  Case 2. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is not closed in $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$;
  Case 3. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed in $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ with finite positive codimension (i.e. $\dim\bigl(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)/M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})\bigr)\in\{1,2,3,\dots\}$);
  Case 4. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed with infinite codimension.

Problem. Determine whether Case 3 can actually occur. Equivalently, decide whether there exists a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ such that the range of $I-2S_-$ on $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ is closed and has finite codimension strictly between $1$ and $\infty$, or prove that no such ideal exists.

Let $D^2$ denote the closed unit disk in $\mathbb{R}^2$ equipped with the Lebesgue (area) measure $\mu$, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$ denote the space of $C^\infty$ diffeomorphisms of $D^2$ that preserve $\mu$, endowed with the $C^\infty$ topology. For $f \in \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$, let $h_\mu(f)$ be the metric (Kolmogorov–Sinai) entropy of $f$ with respect to $\mu$, and let $\lambda_+(f,\mu) = \int \max(\lambda^+(f,x),0)\,d\mu(x)$ be the integrated positive Lyapunov exponent, so that by the Ruelle inequality and Pesin formula one has $h_\mu(f) \le \lambda_+(f,\mu)$, and in the area-preserving setting $h_\mu(f)=0$ if and only if $\lambda_+(f,\mu)=0$. Define
$$G = \{ f \in \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2) : h_\mu(f) = 0 \}.$$
Decide which of the following two mutually exclusive Baire alternatives holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $G$ is a dense $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$;
\item[(b)] there exist a nonempty $C^\infty$-open set $U \subset \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_\mu(D^2)$ and a constant $\delta > 0$ such that $h_\mu(g) \ge \delta$ for every $g \in U$.

Let $M^n$ be a smooth, closed, connected manifold of dimension $n \geq 2$. A diffeomorphism $f: M 	o M$ is called partially hyperbolic if there exists a $Df$-invariant continuous splitting of the tangent bundle
$$TM = E^s \oplus E^c \oplus E^u,$$
with all three subbundles of positive dimension, together with a Riemannian metric on $M$ and continuous positive functions $
u, \hat{
u}, \gamma, \hat{\gamma}$ with $
u, \hat{
u} < 1$ and $
u < \gamma \leq \hat{\gamma}^{-1} < \hat{
u}^{-1}$, such that for every $x \in M$ and unit vectors $v^s \in E^s_x$, $v^c \in E^c_x$, $v^u \in E^u_x$ one has
$$\|Df(x)v^s\| \leq 
u(x), \quad \gamma(x) \leq \|Df(x)v^c\| \leq \hat{\gamma}(x)^{-1}, \quad \hat{
u}(x)^{-1} \leq \|Df(x)v^u\|.$$

Question: Does there exist a closed, connected, simply connected manifold $M^n$ (in some dimension $n$) that admits a partially hyperbolic diffeomorphism?

Consider a 3D bipedal walking robot modeled as a hybrid dynamical system whose continuous phase is governed by Lagrangian dynamics with configuration q in a smooth manifold Q and control input u (joint torques), and whose discrete phase models foot-ground impact via a reset map. A periodic walking gait is a periodic orbit of the closed-loop hybrid system under a feedback controller. Two cost functionals are commonly used to design such controllers by minimizing them over admissible periodic gaits:

(i) Integral-squared torque per step length: J_1 = (1/L) ∫_0^T ||u(t)||^2 dt, where T is the step duration and L the step length.
(ii) Specific cost of transport: J_2 = (∫_0^T P_in(t) dt)/(m g L), where P_in is the input power, m the total mass, and g gravitational acceleration.

Let H denote a reference set of measured human walking gaits (e.g., trajectories of joint angles, ground reaction forces, and center-of-mass motion recorded from healthy human subjects). Define a notion of distance d(γ, H) between a candidate periodic robot gait γ and the reference set H (for instance, a metric on trajectory spaces or on suitable kinematic/dynamic features).

Problem: Construct a cost functional J: {admissible periodic gaits} → ℝ, distinct from J_1 and J_2 and explicitly incorporating a distance d(·, H) from human walking, with the property that the minimizers of J over admissible periodic gaits of the bipedal hybrid model are periodic gaits γ* that are quantifiably closer to H (under d) than the minimizers of J_1 or J_2. Provide:

(a) a precise definition of the metric d on the relevant trajectory/feature space;
(b) the explicit form of J;
(c) a proof or constructive argument that minimizing J yields stable periodic gaits of the hybrid system;
(d) a comparison establishing that minimizers of J are closer to human walking (in d) than minimizers of J_1 and J_2.

Let $M$ be a closed, connected, oriented, irreducible $3$-manifold. Prove or disprove that exactly one of the following three alternatives holds:

(a) $M$ is isometric to a quotient $S^3/\Gamma$, where $S^3$ is the round $3$-sphere and $\Gamma$ is a subgroup of $\mathrm{Isom}(S^3)$ acting freely and properly discontinuously;

(b) the fundamental group $\pi_1(M)$ contains a subgroup isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$;

(c) $M$ is isometric to a quotient $\mathbb{H}^3/\Gamma$, where $\mathbb{H}^3$ is hyperbolic $3$-space and $\Gamma$ is a subgroup of $\mathrm{Isom}(\mathbb{H}^3)$ acting freely and properly discontinuously.

Let S be a semitopological semigroup, i.e., a semigroup endowed with a Hausdorff topology such that for every s ∈ S the maps t ↦ s·t and t ↦ t·s are continuous. Let (E, Q) be a separated locally convex topological vector space whose topology is generated by a saturated family Q of seminorms. An action of S on a subset K of E is a map S × K → K, (s, x) ↦ s·x, satisfying (st)·x = s·(t·x). The action is called:
• separately continuous if for each s ∈ S the map x ↦ s·x is continuous and for each x ∈ K the map s ↦ s·x is continuous;
• weakly separately continuous if the analogous continuity holds with respect to the weak topology of E;
• Q-nonexpansive if q(s·x − s·y) ≤ q(x − y) for every s ∈ S, every q ∈ Q, and all x, y ∈ K;
• weakly equicontinuous if {x ↦ s·x : s ∈ S} is equicontinuous on K with respect to the weak topology;
• weakly quasi-equicontinuous if the closure of {x ↦ s·x : s ∈ S} in the product space K^K endowed with the weak pointwise convergence topology consists of weakly continuous maps.

Define the following fixed point properties of S:
(fppw)  Whenever S acts on a weakly compact convex subset K of a separated locally convex space (E, Q) by a weakly separately continuous, Q-nonexpansive action, K contains a common fixed point for S.
(fppwq) The same conclusion holds whenever the action is moreover weakly quasi-equicontinuous.
(fppwe) The same conclusion holds whenever the action is moreover weakly equicontinuous.
(fppQ)  Whenever S acts on a (norm/Q-)compact convex subset K of a separated locally convex space (E, Q) by a separately continuous, Q-nonexpansive action, K contains a common fixed point for S.

It is known that the implications (fppw) ⇒ (fppwq) ⇒ (fppwe) ⇒ (fppQ) hold for every semitopological semigroup S.

Question: Can any of these three implications be reversed? That is, does there exist a semitopological semigroup S for which one of (fppQ), (fppwe), (fppwq) holds while the next stronger property in the chain fails, or are some (or all) of these four properties in fact equivalent?

Let $S$ be a semitopological semigroup (i.e., a semigroup with a Hausdorff topology under which multiplication is separately continuous). Denote by $C_b(S)$ the space of bounded continuous real-valued functions on $S$, by $AP(S)$ the subspace of almost periodic functions, and by $WAP(S)$ the subspace of weakly almost periodic functions. A mean on a subspace $X \subseteq C_b(S)$ containing the constants is a positive linear functional $m$ with $m(1)=1$; it is left invariant if $m(\ell_s f) = m(f)$ for all $s\in S$ and $f\in X$, where $(\ell_s f)(t)=f(st)$. The semigroup $S$ is left amenable if $C_b(S)$ has a left invariant mean.

Let $H$ be a Hilbert space and $C \subseteq H$ a nonempty closed convex subset. A nonexpansive representation of $S$ on $C$ is a family $\mathcal{T}=\{T_s : s\in S\}$ of mappings $T_s: C\to C$ with $\|T_s x - T_s y\| \le \|x-y\|$ for all $x,y\in C$, satisfying $T_{st}=T_s T_t$ for all $s,t\in S$. The action is weakly jointly continuous if $(s,x)\mapsto T_s x$ is continuous from $S\times (C,\mathrm{wk})$ into $(C,\mathrm{wk})$; weakly separately continuous if it is separately continuous in the same sense; weakly equicontinuous if $\{T_s : s\in S\}$ is equicontinuous on $(C,\mathrm{wk})$; and weakly quasi-equicontinuous if the closure of $\{T_s : s\in S\}$ in $C^C$ with the product weak topology consists of weakly continuous maps.

It is known that the representation $\mathcal{T}$ has a common fixed point in $C$ (i.e., some $x_0\in C$ with $T_s x_0 = x_0$ for all $s\in S$) provided there exists $c\in C$ with bounded orbit $\{T_s c : s\in S\}$ and one of the following holds:
(i) $C_b(S)$ has a left invariant mean and $s\mapsto T_s c$ is continuous from $S$ into $(C,\mathrm{wk})$;
(ii) $S$ is left amenable and the action is weakly jointly continuous;
(iii) $AP(S)$ has a left invariant mean and the action is weakly separately continuous and weakly equicontinuous;
(iv) $WAP(S)$ has a left invariant mean and the action is weakly separately continuous and weakly quasi-equicontinuous.

Question: In each of cases (i)–(iv), does the converse hold? That is, if every nonexpansive representation of $S$ on a nonempty closed convex subset $C$ of a Hilbert space possessing some element with bounded orbit and satisfying the stated continuity/equicontinuity hypothesis admits a common fixed point in $C$, does it follow that the corresponding invariance/amenability hypothesis on $S$ holds?

Background and setup. Over the rationals, the Grothendieck–Ihara program studies the absolute Galois group G_Q = Gal(\bar Q/Q) through its outer action on the étale fundamental group π_1^{ét}(P^1_{\bar Q} \setminus \{0,1,\infty\}). This action produces:
(i) the Ihara power series, a 1-cocycle on G_Q with values in a free pro-l group attached to π_1^{ét}(P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\});
(ii) the Deligne–Soulé cocycles, l-adic Galois cocycles refining the cyclotomic character;
(iii) higher circular (cyclotomic) units, which arise as specializations of these cocycles; and
(iv) the multiple zeta values, realized as periods of mixed Tate motives over Z, with the category of mixed Tate motives MT(Z) controlling all of the above through its motivic fundamental group of P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}.

Now let F_q be a finite field, A = F_q[t], K = F_q(t), and K_\infty its completion at the place at infinity. In this function-field setting one has:
- Carlitz–Drinfeld cyclotomic theory with the v-adic Carlitz cyclotomic character \chi_C : Gal(K^{sep}/K) \to A_v^\times;
- function-field analogues of Ihara power series (Anderson's 1-cocycles I_\sigma satisfying I_{\sigma\tau}(Z) = I_\tau(C_{\chi_C(\sigma)}(Z))\, I_\sigma(Z));
- function-field Deligne–Soulé-type cocycles and higher cyclotomic (Carlitz) units;
- Thakur's multiple zeta values \zeta_A(s_1,\dots,s_d) for A = F_q[t]; and
- a theory of mixed Carlitz–Tate t-motives that provides a period interpretation of these multizeta values via iterated extensions of tensor powers of the Carlitz motive.

Problem. Construct an algebraic/geometric object X over K (analogous to P^1_Q\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}) together with an appropriate notion of fundamental group \Pi(X) — playing the role of the étale or motivic fundamental group — such that:

(a) the outer Galois action of Gal(K^{sep}/K) on \Pi(X) produces the Anderson–Ihara power series and the function-field Deligne–Soulé cocycles as canonical 1-cocycles;
(b) the higher Carlitz cyclotomic units arise as specializations of these cocycles;
(c) the periods of \Pi(X), through a Tannakian / motivic realization compatible with the category of mixed Carlitz–Tate t-motives, yield exactly Thakur's multizeta values \zeta_A(s_1,\dots,s_d) and account for their known relations;
(d) the construction parallels, structurally, the way MT(Z) and \pi_1^{ét}(P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}) jointly control Ihara power series, Deligne–Soulé cocycles, cyclotomic units, and classical multiple zeta values.

Identify such an object X and fundamental-group framework, or prove that none exists with the stated properties.
